  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Ghost Chanterelle (Cantharellus phasmatis) is a pale, ethereal fungus belonging to the Hydnaceae family, characterized by its translucent appearance and delicate structure. This species is found in the forest ecosystems of New England and is of interest to mycologists studying fungal diversity in northeastern North America.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Pale coloration",
      "description": "Distinctive whitish to translucent cap and stem, giving it a ghostly appearance",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Ridge-like structures",
      "description": "Features blunt, forked ridges (not true gills) running down the stem, typical of chanterelles",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Fragile texture",
      "description": "Thin-walled and delicate fruiting body that is easily damaged",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Ghost Chanterelles typically grow on the forest floor in mixed hardwood and coniferous forests throughout New England, preferring moist soil rich in organic matter.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The 'ghost' in its name refers to its unusually pale, translucent appearance, which is rare among chanterelle species and makes it visually distinctive among New England fungi.",
